=== harnez codex-hook
codex-hook implements Codex's PreToolUse command-handler contract for
the "Bash" matcher (see issue 199's Findings). It reads the PreToolUse
JSON payload from stdin and, for a non-empty command not already routed
through 'harnez exec', emits a permissionDecision:"allow" envelope whose
updatedInput.command points at:

  harnez exec --tool <tool_name> -- bash -c '<original command>'

This is the handshake stage only: it never spawns the command or writes
telemetry itself (see docs/HookRewritePattern.md, the same split used by
'harnez exec hook' for Claude Code and 'harnez agy-hooks hook' for agy).
'harnez apply' installs the config.toml entry that invokes this command;
there is no separate 'codex-hooks apply/status' command group.

Usage:
  harnez codex-hook [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for codex-hook

=== harnez codex-telemetry
Usage:
  harnez codex-telemetry [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for codex-telemetry

=== harnez compact
Fast, verbatim agent transcript compaction powered by decision models.

Evaluates historical tool calls and prunes or truncates obsolete outputs while
preserving conversation messages and recent context verbatim.

Usage:
  harnez compact [transcript.jsonl] [flags]

Flags:
      --backend string        backend from spec/decide.yaml (default: jev)
      --format string         output format: jsonl, summary, or json (default "jsonl")
  -h, --help                  help for compact
  -i, --in-place              overwrite the input transcript file in-place
      --max-state-bytes int   maximum byte size for decision request context state (default 102400)
      --model string          model name sent to backend
  -o, --output string         output file (defaults to stdout)
      --pin-recent int        number of recent entries to pin verbatim (default 5)
      --threshold float       decision confidence threshold (0.0 - 1.0) (default 0.5)
  -t, --transcript string     input transcript file (- for stdin)
      --truncate-length int   maximum character length for truncated outputs (default 200)

=== harnez compact-check
compact-check compares a pre-compact transcript snapshot against the
post-compact summary and flags identifiers (file paths, dotted/snake_case
names, etc.) that were mentioned repeatedly before and vanished entirely
after — a cheap, no-LLM-call signal that something load-bearing may have
been lost.

This is a manual command, not automatic watching: Claude Code has a
PreCompact hook but no PostCompact hook, so harnez has no host-native
signal to observe the post-compact result on its own. Save the transcript
before compacting (or point --before at a saved copy) and the summary
after, then run:

  harnez compact-check before.txt after.txt

Usage:
  harnez compact-check <before-file> <after-file> [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for compact-check

=== harnez compose
Print a snippet without a banner. With no name, list available snippets.
Run harnez apply first to install and enable the managed channel plugin.
Channel snippets use the same spec/channel.yaml instructions as channel setup.
The args snippet is shell-quoted; evaluate it to preserve the prompt as one argument.
It also names the session <working-dir-basename>-<4 random chars> (claude -n), so
harnez agent send --name can reach it; a later -n in your own arguments overrides it.

Usage:
  harnez compose [what] [flags]

Examples:
  claude --channels plugin:harnez-channel@harnez-local --append-system-prompt "$(harnez compose claude-channels-prompt)"
  alias claude-channels='claude --channels plugin:harnez-channel@harnez-local --append-system-prompt "$(harnez compose claude-channels-prompt)"'
  claude_channels() {
    eval "command claude $(harnez compose claude-channels-args) \"\$@\""
  }

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for compose

=== harnez config
config reads and changes the per-repo settings in <repo>/.harnez/repo.yaml
without hand-editing YAML.

  harnez config git.mode                  print the effective value
  harnez config git.mode team             set a key
  harnez config --unset git.mode          remove a key
  harnez config --list                    print every effective key=value

Keys are dotted paths (git.mode, issues.tracker, docs.copies, ...). The key
list, value types and allowed values come from the repo.yaml schema. Values
are parsed by type: true/false, an integer, one of the allowed words, or free
text. A list key such as docs.copies takes comma-separated items and replaces
the whole list (docs.copies golang,bash; an empty value gives an empty list).
A negative number needs -- before it, as in: harnez config <key> -- -1.

A set or unset is checked with the same rules as loading repo.yaml (schema,
required keys, git.protected_main needs git.mode team or fork). When a check
fails nothing is written and the error names the key and the allowed values.
Comments, key order and unrelated keys survive the edit.

--list shows spec defaults for keys the file leaves out, marked (default).
After a set or unset of a key that changes rendered output (AGENTS.md, rules,
Makefile), config prints "run: harnez init -d <repo>"; it does not run init.
get and --list also work without a repo.yaml; set and unset need one.

Usage:
  harnez config [-d <repo>] (<key> [<value>] | --unset <key> | --list) [flags]

Flags:
  -d, --dir string   repo root containing .harnez/repo.yaml (default ".")
  -h, --help         help for config
      --list         print every effective key=value, spec defaults marked (default)
      --unset        remove the key

=== harnez decide
Ask a decision model typed questions about a state.

A request is a state plus named questions, from a spec file (-f, YAML or JSON,
"-" for stdin) or from one quick question (--noul, --choice or --score).
The state comes from --state (JSON or text), --state-file, the arguments,
or stdin, in that order; any of these replaces a state in the -f file, and
stdin is not read when the file has one.

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 --threshold not met, 2 error.

Backends are defined in spec/decide.yaml; the default is Jev, which needs
$TYPESAFE_API_KEY.

Usage:
  harnez decide [state text...] [flags]

Examples:
  harnez decide --noul "Does this need human confirmation?" \
    --option "true: destructive or rewrites remote history" --option "false: safe" \
    --state '{"command": "git push --force"}' --threshold 0.5

  harnez decide --choice "Classify the risk" --option "safe: read-only" \
    --option "danger: deletes data" --pick q "rm -rf build"

  harnez decide --score "How severe?" --level cosmetic --level degraded --level blocker \
    --state-file issue.md

  harnez decide -f review-gate.yaml --format json

Flags:
      --backend string       backend from spec/decide.yaml (default: its default_backend)
      --choice string        quick choice question; one --option name: description per choice
  -f, --file string          request spec file (YAML or JSON, - for stdin)
      --format string        output: table, json, quiet (default "table")
  -h, --help                 help for decide
      --id string            question id for --noul/--choice/--score (default "q")
      --level stringArray    score level description (repeatable)
      --model string         model name sent to the backend
      --noul string          quick yes/no question; --option true: ..., false: ...
      --option stringArray   criterion "name: description" (repeatable)
      --pick string          print only this question's answer (as JSON with --format json)
      --score string         quick score question; one --level per rubric level, lowest first
      --state string         state as JSON, or plain text
      --state-file string    read the state as text from a file
      --threshold float      exit 1 unless the answer's level is >= this (noul/choice probability, score level)

=== harnez diff
Show what apply would change in managed blocks

Usage:
  harnez diff [flags]

Flags:
      --capture-docs         write configured project-doc drift to an inbox Markdown report
      --components strings   component(s) to inspect, comma-separated or repeated (e.g. docs-only,telemetry)
  -c, --config string        path to config YAML file (default: embedded)
  -e, --exit-code            exit with status 1 if drift/changes are found
  -h, --help                 help for diff
      --out string           output path for --capture-docs (default: issues/inbox/managed-docs-drift-<timestamp>.md)
  -t, --target string        Claude config directory (default: ~/.claude)

=== harnez distill
distill filters routine command output down to the signal an agent needs.

Streaming mode reads stdin:
  go test ./... | harnez distill

Wrapper mode runs a command, distills its combined output, and exits with
the wrapped command's exit code:
  harnez distill -- go test ./...
  harnez distill -- git status

Usage:
  harnez distill [-- command args...] [flags]
  harnez distill [command]

Available Commands:
  hook        PreToolUse hook: auto-pipe noisy Bash commands through distill
  read        Bound authorized native read text or prepare attachable PNG output (JSON stdin)

Flags:
  -h, --help            help for distill
      --max-bytes int   hard byte cap on output; truncates head+tail with a note when exceeded (0 disables) (default 200000)
      --max-lines int   truncate output beyond this many lines (0 disables) (default 300)
      --mode string     filter mode: auto, gotest, git, raw (default "auto")
      --no-dedup        skip collapsing repeated lines

Use "harnez distill [command] --help" for more information about a command.

=== harnez distill hook
hook implements a Claude Code PreToolUse hook for the Bash matcher.

It is a no-op unless HARNEZ_DISTILL_AUTOPIPE is set to "1" or "true": harnez apply
can install this hook globally without it changing anything until a user
opts in. When enabled, it rewrites known-noisy commands (go test/build/vet,
cargo test/build, pytest, npm test, make test/build/check, git status/diff/log)
to pipe their combined output through 'harnez distill', preserving the
original command's exit code via 'set -o pipefail'.

Usage:
  harnez distill hook [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for hook

=== harnez distill read
Bound authorized native read text or prepare attachable PNG output (JSON stdin)

Usage:
  harnez distill read [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for read

=== harnez dochistory
dochistory analyzes Git history, tracking token evolution,
metrics, multi-track categories (code, tests, docs, skills, issues), and distributions across commits.

Invocations:
  harnez dochistory                    # Analyze default managed documentation (docs/ and AGENTS.md)
  harnez dochistory --tracks           # Multi-track repository evolution sparks (code, tests, docs, skills, issues)
  harnez dochistory --tracks --diff    # Expanded additions and removals breakdown table
  harnez dochistory docs/lang/Go.md    # Single-file timeline and token sparkline
  harnez dochistory docs/ issues       # Multi-directory / glob stacked category breakdown
  harnez dochistory --json             # Machine-readable JSON output

Usage:
  harnez dochistory [files...] [flags]

Aliases:
  dochistory, doc-history, repo-history

Flags:
      --color        enable ANSI color output (default true)
      --diff         show additions and removals breakdown in multi-track history
  -d, --dir string   repository root directory (default: current directory or git root)
  -h, --help         help for dochistory
      --json         output history in JSON format
      --no-color     disable ANSI color output
      --tracks       analyze multi-track repository evolution (code, tests, docs, skills, issues)

