=== harnez index
index regenerates two hand-maintained index tables from the files that are
their actual source of truth, so they stop drifting:

  issues/README.md   <- active Open/In Progress/Blocked/Draft tickets
  issues/BACKLOG.md  <- Backlog tickets
  issues/CLOSED.md   <- Closed tickets, including archived tickets
  docs/README.md      <- docs/studies/*.md (docs/studies/ table only)

It is idempotent: unchanged sources report no changes. Issue indexes run only
when issues.tracker: harnez is set in .harnez/repo.yaml; docs.studies: true
enables the studies index independently. If neither configured index is
present, it prints a note and exits successfully.
Pass --check to fail (exit 1) instead of writing, for CI/pre-commit use --
mirrors 'harnez diff --exit-code' (issue 037). --check also prints a unified
diff of the drift, so the output is self-contained enough for an agent
running the command directly in-session to see exactly what changed and
fix the offending ticket(s) without a separate diff step. Pass --stage to
stage only index files rewritten by this run; it requires a Git repository.
When no index file changes, --stage stages nothing.

Each run that isn't --check also appends a dated open/closed/draft/unknown
ticket count snapshot for this repo to the shared telemetry DB (issue 228),
deduped against the project's most recently recorded snapshot so repeated
no-op runs don't grow the history unboundedly. Snapshot-write failures are
logged (DEBUG=1) and otherwise swallowed -- they never fail this command.
View recorded snapshots with 'harnez find issues history'.

Usage:
  harnez index [flags]

Flags:
      --check        report drift without writing; exit 1 if regeneration would change a file
  -d, --dir string   repo root containing issues/ and docs/ (default ".")
  -h, --help         help for index
      --stage        stage only index files rewritten by this run

=== harnez init
Set up a project directory with AGENTS.md, language docs, and Makefile targets

Usage:
  harnez init [flags]

Flags:
      --all                treat --dir as a workspace directory and non-interactively init every eligible child (has AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md); refuses $HOME (see issue 068)
  -c, --config string      path to config YAML file (default: embedded)
  -d, --dir string         project directory to initialise (default: current directory) (default ".")
      --docs strings       docs to set up in the project, comma-separated or repeated (e.g. golang,canary)
  -f, --force              allow initializing home directory, root, or non-coding directory
      --git-mode string    alias for --repo-mode
      --gowork             set up or migrate Go workspace (go.work.example + untracked local go.work symlink)
  -h, --help               help for init
      --issues-git         record issue-index Git integration for a Harnez tracker (use --issues-git=false to remove it)
      --json               output RAMP report in JSON format
      --no-wizard          skip repository feature prompts
      --protected-main     record git.protected_main: harnez refuses commits and pushes on the default branch (needs team or fork)
      --quota-1            scaffold Quota-1 single-test guardrails in AGENTS.md and Makefile
      --ramp               report RAMP maturity profile and projected init changes (offline, read-only)
      --ramp-report        alias for --ramp
      --replace            delete existing AGENTS.md and recreate from template before init
  -m, --repo-mode string   repo git workflow, recorded as git.mode in .harnez/repo.yaml and noted in AGENTS.md (solo, fork, team)
      --summary            run claude -p to generate a project summary and add it to AGENTS.md
      --update             re-fetch and refresh the project summary (implies --summary)
      --variant string     doc variant to install: lite or full (default: keep each existing doc variant, else full)
      --wizard             prompt for repository features even without a terminal (EOF keeps defaults)
  -y, --yes                use detected/recorded setup and reconcile Makefile targets without prompts

=== harnez install
Install an optional dependency and run its bootstrap

Usage:
  harnez install <name> [flags]

Flags:
      --dry-run   Print commands without installing
  -h, --help      help for install

=== harnez issues
issues collapses today's four-step ticket-status-change dance (hand-edit the
'**Status**:' line, run 'harnez index', 'git add' the changed files, 'git
commit') into one call: it rewrites the ticket's Status line, regenerates
issues/README.md, BACKLOG.md, and CLOSED.md in-process (the same logic
'harnez index' uses), and commits the changed files by default.

Verbs (closed set, mirroring docs/IssueTracking.md's Allowed Values):

  open [reason]    Status: Open, or "Open — <reason>"
  start [reason]   Status: In Progress, or "In Progress — <reason>"
  block <reason>   Status: "Blocked — <reason>" (reason required)
  close [reason]   Status: Closed (bare), or "Closed — <reason>"
  done [reason]    Status: Closed (bare), or "Closed — <reason>" (alias for close)
  draft [reason]   Status: Draft, or "Draft — <reason>"
  backlog [reason] Status: Backlog, or "Backlog — <reason>"
  promote          Move a Backlog ticket back to Open

Status verbs accept one or more ticket numbers. Leading numeric or GH-prefixed arguments
are targets; the first non-number argument starts the reason. A numeric-only
reason is therefore ambiguous: include text in the reason (for example,
'"123 tickets"'). Batch targets are all validated before any file is changed,
then the README is regenerated and the batch is committed once.
  new --remote [--force] "title" Create a GitHub issue on the github remote and reserve
                    its permanent GH<number> ID. Requires authenticated gh;
                    also selected when CLAUDE_CODE_REMOTE is set. Strong
                    duplicate matches block before gh issue create unless
                    --force is supplied. No fallback.
  new [--force] [--number N] [title]
                    Atomically reserve the next free issue number and create
                    a Draft placeholder file (issues/<NNN>-<title-slug>.md,
                    or issues/<NNN>-reserved.md with no title), using
                    O_CREATE|O_EXCL so concurrent callers never collide. Non-
                    JSON output is "<NUMBER>\t<PATH>" -- write the ticket's
                    real content directly to that printed path instead of
                    re-deriving the slug from the title by hand (a
                    hand-derived slug can diverge and leave an orphaned
                    placeholder behind, see issue 202). Strong duplicate
                    matches block unless --force is supplied. --number N uses
                    ticket number N (e.g. one reserved on another machine)
                    instead of the next free one; it fails if a ticket with
                    that number already exists. Unlike every
                    other verb, 'new' takes no ticket number (there isn't one
                    yet) and never commits.
  show <number>    Inspect a ticket. In interactive terminal / TTY or with
                    -I/--image, renders a bounded visual card (using
                    internal/readcard). Supports --raw/--text and --json.
  mv <old> [new]   Renumber a ticket to [new] (default: next free number
                    from scanning issuesDir). Renames the file keeping the
                    same slug, rewrites the '# <new> — <title>' header line,
                    resyncs issues/README.md, and commits both files by default.
                    Guarded by O_CREATE|O_EXCL so concurrent claims or existing
                    tickets are never overwritten.
  rebase [upstream] Replay local commits onto upstream, deriving local ticket
                    ownership from history and deterministically repairing any
                    number collisions in an explicit final commit. --dry-run
                    prints the plan without changing Git state.
  reconcile [ref]   After a git merge, renumber this branch's own colliding
                    tickets (added in ref..HEAD; default: merged parent, else
                    origin/main), rewrite references, repair indexes and commit
                    once. --dry-run prints the plan. --fetch fetches and prints
                    the plan against the upstream; --pull fetches, merges the
                    upstream and reconciles (use instead of git pull).
  lint              Read-only validation for duplicate numbers, conflicting
                    filename/H1 numbers, and drift across all three indexes.
  status            Show lifecycle counts and warn when active tickets exceed
                    the configured spec cap.
  list [filter]     Read-only: list tickets matching [filter] (default:
                    "is:open"), a thin wrapper over 'harnez find issues' --
                    not a status-mutation verb, and does not accept
                    --check/--dry-run/--commit/--no-commit. Supports the
                    same -n/--limit/--all flags as 'find', plus the bare
                    git-log-style '-N' shorthand (e.g. 'issues list -3').

'close' with no reason writes bare "Closed", never an auto-fabricated
"Closed — resolved" -- both are common in the corpus and this command does
not guess which the caller means. It also never guesses a commit sha for a
"resolved in <sha>" reason (that sha does not exist until this command's own
commit is made) -- supply it explicitly, or correct it with --amend-reason.

For close and done, --model and --session record optional attribution directly
under Status as **Closed-By**. They default to HARNEZ_AGENT_MODEL and
HARNEZ_SESSION_ID; explicit flags take precedence. Missing values are omitted,
and generated indexes do not include attribution.

Close gate: close and done refuse unless a commit with changes outside issues/
references the ticket number, such as a subject ending in "(issue 42)". Commits
that touch only issues/ do not count, and a near-miss number does not match (86
does not match 867). For a ticket that needed no code, pass --no-code: the close
goes through and its commit body says so. Batch targets are checked before any
file is written.

Idempotency: re-running a verb that would produce the exact same Status
line text already on disk is a no-op -- exit 0, no file/README/commit
changes. Closing an already-Closed ticket is always a no-op, regardless of
the requested reason. Use close (or done) --amend-reason to explicitly
correct its reason in one commit; this requires an already-Closed ticket.
Other verbs still allow reason changes on an already-matching status.

--check (alias --dry-run) reports what would change without writing or
committing, and exits 1 if there is drift from the requested state (mirrors
'harnez index --check').

A nonexistent or ambiguous ticket number is a caller-bug error (non-zero
exit, actionable stderr) -- unlike 'harnez find', where zero matches is a
valid, exit-0 answer.

Usage:
  harnez issues <verb> <ticket-number...> [reason...] [flags]

Flags:
  -a, --all              with 'list': show all matching results
      --amend-reason     with close/done: amend the reason of an already-Closed ticket
      --cached           validate the staged Git snapshot (issues lint only)
      --check            report what would change without writing or committing; exit 1 on drift
      --commit string    override the default commit message (default: "docs(issues): <verb> <ticket-number>[, <reason>]")
  -d, --dir string       repo root containing issues/ (default ".")
      --dry-run          alias for --check
      --fetch            reconcile: git fetch, then print the plan against the upstream (no changes)
      --force            with new: create despite strong duplicate matches
  -h, --help             help for issues
  -I, --image            render visual PNG card context
      --json             output a single JSON object instead of a text line
  -n, --limit int        with 'list': limit results (default: newest 10) (default 10)
  -l, --list             list open issues (alias for the list verb)
      --model string     with close/done: record the closing model (defaults to HARNEZ_AGENT_MODEL)
      --no-code          with close/done: the ticket needed no code change, so skip the commit check and record that in the close commit
      --no-commit        rewrite the ticket file and README but do not git add/commit
      --number string    with new: use this ticket number instead of the next free one (refused if it exists)
      --pull             reconcile: git fetch + merge the upstream, then renumber colliding local tickets and commit once
  -r, --raw              output raw text directly to stdout
      --remote           with new: allocate a permanent GH id using authenticated gh on the github remote
      --session string   with close/done: record the closing session (defaults to HARNEZ_SESSION_ID)
  -t, --text             output raw text directly to stdout (alias for --raw)

=== harnez lint
lint verifies source files, scripts, and documentation against harnez
standards and invariants (e.g. Bash 3-line conditionals and source-over-dot,
Markdown and Makefile marker integrity).

With no arguments, lint checks every git-tracked file it recognises; a
directory argument checks the tracked files below it. Explicit files are
linted even when untracked.

Language is automatically inferred from file extensions or shebangs, or can
be overridden explicitly with --lang. In directory mode, --lang selects only
files of that language.

Without --check, lint reports and exits 0. --check fails on errors;
--check=warning also fails on warnings.

Errors are always listed. When linting directories (or the whole repo),
warnings are summarised by rule and by the files with the most warnings;
--verbose lists every warning. --json always includes every finding.

Usage:
  harnez lint [flags] [file|dir]...

Examples:
  harnez lint                      # all tracked files
  harnez lint --check scripts docs # gate on errors below two dirs
  harnez lint --check=warning --lang go

Flags:
      --check string[="error"]   exit non-zero on findings at or above this level (error, warning)
  -h, --help                     help for lint
      --json                     output findings as JSON
      --lang string              language override (auto, bash, go, make, markdown) (default "auto")
  -v, --verbose                  list every warning instead of a summary in directory mode

=== harnez load-stream
Stream local CPU/GPU load samples as NDJSON (internal: driven remotely by usage --watch, issue 110)

Usage:
  harnez load-stream [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for load-stream

=== harnez log
log lists what harnez itself has run — which subcommand, when, in which
project, by whom, and whether it succeeded — newest first, in the shape of
'git log':

  harnez log            # last 20 invocations in the current project
  harnez log -5         # last 5, exactly like 'git log -5'
  harnez log --failed   # only invocations that exited non-zero
  harnez log --human    # only invocations a person typed at a terminal

Rows come from the cli_invocations telemetry table, written automatically
around every harnez run (issue 326). With no arguments, output is scoped to
the project inferred from the current directory and falls back to all
projects when the current directory is not one harnez has recorded.

This command answers exactly one question: what did harnez run, and when.
It deliberately takes no subcommands, because the neighbouring questions
already have their own commands and must not be duplicated here:

  harnez stats                # aggregates over tool_calls: failure rates,
                              # scores, distillation byte savings
  harnez find issues history  # how open/closed ticket counts have moved
  harnez dochistory           # how managed docs have evolved
  git log -- docs/ issues/    # what actually changed in the repo's docs and
                              # tickets (they live in the git tree by design)

--agent accepts either a bare id ('claude') or the stored form
('agent:claude'). --human and --agent are mutually exclusive. --all uncaps
the result and drops the current-directory project scoping; an explicit
--project still applies.

Usage:
  harnez log [-N | -n <limit>] [--project <name>] [--command <name>] [--failed] [--json] [flags]

Flags:
      --agent string     filter to one agent (e.g. claude, or the stored agent:claude)
      --all              show every recorded invocation across all projects (uncapped)
      --auto             filter to the current session, resolved from the environment (like harnez stats --auto)
      --command string   filter to one subcommand (the full path, e.g. 'index' or 'issues new')
      --dir string       directory whose project name scopes the default view (default: cwd)
      --failed           only invocations that exited non-zero
  -h, --help             help for log
      --human            filter to invocations attributed to a human at a terminal
      --json             output the rows as JSON instead of a formatted table
  -n, --limit int        maximum number of invocations to show (also: bare -N, like git log -5) (default 20)
      --project string   filter to one project_name
      --session string   filter to one session_id
      --since duration   only invocations newer than this duration ago (e.g. 2h, 48h)

=== harnez man
Generate, print, or install roff man pages

Usage:
  harnez man [flags]

Flags:
      --dir string   custom target directory for man pages (implies --install)
  -h, --help         help for man
      --install      install man pages into the standard man directory

=== harnez mcp
Serve Harnez agent tools over MCP stdio

Usage:
  harnez mcp [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for mcp

=== harnez mode
mode dynamically switches the operational terseness level for the active session
and updates the managed 'Concise Mode' section in ./.harnez/rules/Local.md.

Tiers:
  lite (1)        Level 1: Concise Lite (Professional Terse, no pleasantries)
  std (2)         Level 2: Concise Standard (Telegraphic fragments, zero filler)
  ultra (3)       Level 3: Concise Ultra (Diffs/status only, zero narrative)
  vision (4)      Vision Mode: Multimodal visual cheatsheet context (@docs/vision/*.png)
  off (0, reset)  Disable ConciseMode / reset local overlay

Flags:
  -e, --ephemeral, --no-file  Emit stdout directive only; do not write any file to disk
	      --persist, --main       Accepted for compatibility; Local.md remains the target
	-f, --file                  Target instructions file (default: ./.harnez/rules/Local.md)
  -q, --quiet                 Suppress in-flight LLM directive on stdout (file update only)
      --dry-run               Show directive and changes without writing to disk

Usage:
  harnez mode [tier] [flags]
  harnez mode [command]

Aliases:
  mode, concise

Available Commands:
  autonomous-read Allow native reads and observe their opportunity cost
  enforce-read    Enable native large-read enforcement
  lite            Switch to Concise Lite (Level 1)
  off             Disable ConciseMode / remove overlay directive
  std             Switch to Concise Standard (Level 2)
  ultra           Switch to Concise Ultra (Level 3)
  vision          Switch to Vision Mode (Multimodal visual cards)

Flags:
      --dry-run       display directive and planned file changes without modifying disk
  -e, --ephemeral     emit stdout directive only; do not write any file to disk
  -f, --file string   target instructions file (default: ./.harnez/rules/Local.md)
  -h, --help          help for mode
      --main          alias for --persist
      --no-file       alias for --ephemeral
      --persist       accepted for compatibility; still write to ./.harnez/rules/Local.md
  -q, --quiet         suppress stdout directive (file sync only)

Use "harnez mode [command] --help" for more information about a command.

=== harnez mode autonomous-read
Allow native reads and observe their opportunity cost

Usage:
