=== harnez rate
rate writes a single tool_calls row (call_type=internal, exit_code=NULL)
recording how well an internal tool call (file read, edit, semantic scan,
web search, ...) served the agent's purpose. Designed to fire immediately
after every such tool call without MCP/JSON-RPC schema overhead:

  harnez rate <tool_name> <score> "<description>" [<ticket_id>]

  tool_name    free-form tool identifier, e.g. "Read" or "semantic-scan"
  score        integer 1-5
  description  one-line quoted summary
  ticket_id    optional "<project_folder>/<ticket_name>"; when omitted it
               is resolved from the current git branch (or the session's
               most recently used ticket) instead of being written as NULL

Sub-20ms end-to-end target: this fires many times per agent turn.

--ok records a distinct, lighter-weight heartbeat instead of a per-tool
rating (call_type=heartbeat, score=NULL, exit_code=NULL) — issue 179's
answer to "silence is ambiguous": confirm a stretch of tool calls was fine
without inventing a fake per-tool score for it. No tool_name/score
required:

  harnez rate --ok ["<note>"] [<ticket_id>] [--since <n>]

  note       optional one-line note (defaults to "ok")
  ticket_id  optional, same resolution as the default form
  --since n  optional: how many tool calls this heartbeat covers (recorded
             in the note for human reference only; not a queryable column)

Usage:
  harnez rate <tool_name> <score> "<description>" [<ticket_id>] [flags]

Flags:
      --agent string     agent identifier override (default: $HARNEZ_AGENT or auto-detect)
  -h, --help             help for rate
      --ok               record a lean heartbeat confirming recent tool calls were fine, instead of a per-tool rating
      --session string   session_id override (default: resolve.Session())
      --since int        with --ok: number of tool calls this heartbeat covers (descriptive only)

=== harnez read
Read files or stdin as text, or PNG context cards with -I.
--auto is retained as a text-only compatibility flag; it never selects images.
Use -I explicitly to render PNG context cards.
Explicit -I forces images; --text and --raw cannot be combined with -I.
Images pack up to three columns, pruning unused columns and cropping to content.

--line-numbers=all|off|N controls the gutter cadence and preserves source anchors.
--compress=ws|ast safely compacts Go and JSON. Shell uses conservative lexical
compaction in both modes; complex expansions and heredocs remain verbatim.
Compression requires complete valid Go/JSON input; source files are never changed.

Examples:
  harnez read -n -L 10:50 internal/lint/lint.go
  harnez read -I --line-numbers=10 --compress=ws internal/lint/lint.go
  harnez read --head=100 internal/lint/lint.go
  harnez read -I --columns=2 --json source.go

Usage:
  harnez read [flags] [files...]

Flags:
      --auto                  compatibility flag; always use text
      --chrome string         card decoration: full, slim or none (title moves into the meta box)
  -c, --columns int           maximum columns (1-4; unused columns pruned) (default 3)
      --compress string       safe source compression: off, ws, ast (default "off")
      --font string           font: pixel, retro, 5x8, 3x5, micro, 6x12, standard, 8x16, 7x13 (default "pixel")
      --font-size int         font size in pixels (default 11)
      --frame string          sections (diff hunks, headings, Go funcs): off, sep (lines) or box
      --gutter string         line numbers: normal, tight (micro font) or sup (micro font, top-aligned)
      --head int              read only the first N lines
  -h, --help                  help for read
  -I, --image                 force styled PNG output
      --json                  structured JSON metadata
      --line-numbers string   gutter: all, off, none, or positive cadence N (default "all")
  -L, --lines string          source line range, e.g. 10:50
      --max-dim int           maximum image dimension in pixels (default 1568)
      --meta string           info box in free top-right space: off or box (red dotted)
  -n, --number                force text output with line numbers (unless -I)
  -o, --out string            output PNG file or existing directory
      --raw                   force text without line numbers
      --stats                 alias for --tokens
      --style string          card style preset: default, compact (slim, tight, sep, box) or max (none, sup, box, box) (default "default")
      --tail int              read only the last N lines
      --text                  force text output
      --theme string          color theme: dark, light (default "dark")
      --tokens                show token estimates
      --wrap string           line wrapping: soft, truncate (default "soft")

=== harnez release
Automates the full project release lifecycle:
  1. Reads or initializes the single source of truth version.yaml spec.
  2. Bumps semver (patch, minor, major, or explicit version) or resumes (--continue).
  3. Propagates version to language files (version.go, __version__.py, build.zig.zon, etc.).
  4. Runs GoReleaser or custom build command to produce dist/ artifacts.
  5. Cryptographically signs SHA256SUMS with minisign (non-interactive).
  6. Verifies/enables Forgejo/Codeberg repository 'has_releases' unit via API.
  7. Tags and pushes git branch and tags to remote origin.
  8. Publishes release and attaches artifacts to Codeberg/Forgejo via fj.

The build step runs with GOWORK=off by default, so an enclosing go.work
file (e.g. sibling modules checked out for local co-development) never
substitutes untagged local sources for a pinned go.mod/go.sum dependency.
Pass --allow-workspace to opt back in when that's genuinely intended.

Usage:
  harnez release [flags]

Flags:
      --allow-workspace     honor an enclosing go.work file during the build step (default: GOWORK=off, so the release always builds against pinned, tagged go.mod/go.sum deps, not untagged local workspace sources)
      --build-cmd string    custom build command (defaults to goreleaser or make dist)
  -b, --bump string         version bump type ('patch', 'minor', 'major', or explicit semver 'x.y.z') (default "patch")
      --continue            resume a previous release safely without bumping or re-tagging
  -d, --dir string          target project directory (default ".")
  -n, --dry-run             simulate execution without modifying files, git, or remote forge
      --force               re-release the current code as a new version even if nothing changed since the previous tag
  -h, --help                help for release
  -s, --sign-key string     path to secret minisign key (defaults to ~/.minisign/<project>.key)
      --skip-build          skip artifact compilation/packaging
      --skip-publish        skip publishing artifacts to forge via fj
      --skip-push           skip pushing git commit and tags to remote
      --skip-sign           skip minisign cryptographic signing
      --tag-prefix string   override git tag prefix (defaults to tag_prefix in version.yaml or 'v')

=== harnez repo-history
dochistory analyzes Git history, tracking token evolution,
metrics, multi-track categories (code, tests, docs, skills, issues), and distributions across commits.

Invocations:
  harnez dochistory                    # Analyze default managed documentation (docs/ and AGENTS.md)
  harnez dochistory --tracks           # Multi-track repository evolution sparks (code, tests, docs, skills, issues)
  harnez dochistory --tracks --diff    # Expanded additions and removals breakdown table
  harnez dochistory docs/lang/Go.md    # Single-file timeline and token sparkline
  harnez dochistory docs/ issues       # Multi-directory / glob stacked category breakdown
  harnez dochistory --json             # Machine-readable JSON output

Usage:
  harnez dochistory [files...] [flags]

Aliases:
  dochistory, doc-history, repo-history

Flags:
      --color        enable ANSI color output (default true)
      --diff         show additions and removals breakdown in multi-track history
  -d, --dir string   repository root directory (default: current directory or git root)
  -h, --help         help for dochistory
      --json         output history in JSON format
      --no-color     disable ANSI color output
      --tracks       analyze multi-track repository evolution (code, tests, docs, skills, issues)

=== harnez repo-status
repo-status summarizes git working-tree state using
'git status --porcelain=v2 --branch' (machine-parseable, not scraped
human-readable text).

Quiet path (default): the tree is clean -- nothing staged, unstaged, or
untracked, no conflicts, HEAD not detached, and not behind the upstream --
prints one short line, e.g. "clean, up to date" or "clean, 3 ahead of
origin/main". Being ahead of the upstream alone does NOT trigger the
verbose path: unpushed local commits are the expected normal state in a
solo/no-PR-workflow repo, not something worth narrating on every check.

Verbose path: any staged/unstaged/untracked file, merge conflict, detached
HEAD, or being behind the upstream by any amount (including a full
ahead-and-behind divergence) prints a short structured summary -- counts
by category, branch divergence, and the affected file/conflict paths --
instead of the full raw 'git status' dump.

Usage:
  harnez repo-status [flags]

Flags:
  -d, --dir string   git repo directory to inspect (default ".")
  -h, --help         help for repo-status

